#chapter(title: "Conclusion", tagline: [In which the chill is gone, and we count what we built.])

== What we built

Eight stages ago, `Main` created two `Candidate`s and printed them, and there were four things it could not do. Here is where those four ended up.

_Describe a class without reading its source._ Stage 01 asked a `Class` object about itself; stage 02's `Inspector` listed every constructor, field, method and parameter of any class, and stage 03 taught it to list annotations it had never heard of. In stage 07 the same idea went one level down, to the instructions inside the methods.

_Reach private members._ Stage 02 did, with `setAccessible(true)`, and found the one wall the JVM puts in the way: its own modules, since Java 16. Every later stage used that door, the validator, the test runner, the container, without a second thought, which is exactly how frameworks use it.

_Create an instance of a class named at run time._ Stage 01 opened the door with `Class.forName`; stage 05 walked through it with configuration, `ServiceLoader`, a compiler called from inside the program and a loader of our own, and discovered that a class is identified by its name and its loader together.

_Decorate, replace or wire without editing `Main`._ Stage 04 wrapped `Candidate` in logging, validation, transactions and a network stub without touching it, using classes generated by `Proxy`; stage 06 took the wiring away from `Main` altogether, with a container that reads annotations and builds the object graph; stage 07 generated the subclass that `Proxy` could not.

Along the way we wrote, in a few hundred lines in total, a recognisable fraction of Bean Validation, JUnit, Spring AOP, RMI, JPA's lazy loading, `ServiceLoader`, a Spring-style `ApplicationContext`, Guava's `EventBus` and CGLIB. None of it is production quality, and all of it is the real mechanism.

== The one idea

If the book has a single thesis, it is the one from stage 00: in plain Java every decision about which class, which method and which field is made by the compiler from source text, and meta-programming is the art of making some of those decisions _later_, with information that only exists later.

Reflection makes the decision at run time, from names. Annotations let the code being decided about have a say. Proxies and generated classes make the decision for code that does not exist yet. Class loaders decide where the code comes from at all. Inversion of control is what it looks like when a framework makes all of these decisions and the application only declares. Each later stage is the same move, applied to a different decision.

The same thesis explains the current direction of the industry. GraalVM native image, Spring AOT, Quarkus and Micronaut are moving decisions _back_ towards build time, because startup time and memory have become expensive in containers and serverless functions. They do not abandon the mechanisms of this book; they run them earlier and record the results. The annotations are the same, the proxies are generated at build time, the container's wiring is written out as code. Understanding the run-time version is the only way to understand what the build-time version is doing on your behalf.

== What it costs, in one place

Every chapter had a bill, and the bills rhyme:

- *Names as strings.* No compiler check, no refactoring support, errors at run time or at startup instead of at compile time.
- *Speed.* A reflective call is an order of magnitude slower than a direct one. Reflect once, cache, call through something cheaper.
- *Encapsulation.* Reflection defeats it; the JDK and your own modules can push back.
- *Visibility.* Behaviour happens in handlers, containers and generated classes that are written nowhere near the code they affect. "Go to definition" stops working; stack traces grow.
- *Trust.* A loaded class is executed code, and Log4Shell is what happens when that is forgotten.
- *Closed worlds.* Everything decided at run time must be declared in advance for ahead-of-time compilation.

None of these is a reason not to use frameworks. All of them are reasons to know what a framework is doing, which was the point.

== What to do next

Run the stages. Change them. The exercises at the end of each chapter are small on purpose: adding `@Primary` to the container or a `CachingHandler` to the proxy chain takes an hour and teaches more than reading the chapter twice.

Then open a real framework. Find `AopUtils.getTargetClass` in Spring and recognise `Proxies.unwrap`. Find `ServiceLoader` in a JDBC driver's jar. Put a breakpoint in a `@Transactional` method and read the stack trace from the bottom up: `$Proxy` or `$$SpringCGLIB$$`, an interceptor chain, a transaction manager, your code. You wrote each of those frames once. The chill should be gone.

